 "director": {
  "mode": "hand",
  "notes": "Sorcery, ropes, staffs and snakes are never drawn. Musa and Harun get no motif; no motif in 80-81 and 84 (Musa's words). Pharaoh's order is framed («معارضًا للحق… ومغالطًا»). 83:1-7 keeps Al-Muyassar's general wording (As-Sa'di's «شباب» is not used). Light for «ويحق الله الحق». STAGING v4: The gathering in Egypt, by day (city setting, no pyramids), as in Al-A'raf. Musa is a blue drop labelled Musa (no plate on his name; the camera is wide whenever he is named or speaks: no close-up or pan on his drop, no close-up on others during his words); Harun is not named in 79-84 and is not drawn. Pharaoh is a plain faceless king. c1 (79-82): Pharaoh speaks his order (79:1), the camera on him (79:3); the magicians walk in on 80:2, three faceless men in dark robes (the series' sign for a group, no number meant: their number is not in the sources; not an uncountable crowd, whose rocks read as idols or troops), the camera on them (80:3). Musa speaks (80:7, 81:5, 81:9, 81:12). Their throwing (81:1-2) is never drawn: no ropes, staffs, snakes, light or glow; the camera only holds on the magicians. A geometric plate for Allah establishes the truth by His words (82:1-7). As-Sa'di's mention of the magicians' prostration is not drawn (not in these verses). c2 (83-84): the few of Musa's people who believed (both tafsirs: offspring, young men of the Children of Israel) are three believers in light robes on the right, apart from Musa's drop so that a close-up on them never enlarges or crops it, the camera on them (83:5, 83:12); Pharaoh and his chiefs are not on the stage (the believers fear them, they are not shown confronting them): a crown plate stands for Pharaoh, haughty in the land (83:11 to 84:1). Musa speaks to them (84:1); his conditional words (84:4-7) are not acted; a seal plate for rely upon Him (84:8-12)."
 },
